    "q-141882": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141882",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/82a08c804381b11ae118691d82c4801b655cd8e0b131677f67ed6e63b8400d7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[2,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[2,6]$ given that $f(2)=-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
