    "q-141619": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141619",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/aa70775eb1b2968406c61e1bebad52139f492890e71a9977582fc1898ba5ee8f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\n We see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-7\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
