    "q-343672": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343672",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/c5f0d970e95b7e2e2656ef579f9d5be49eb9eddb433a5921149347ef349139c5.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $6$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $g(-3)=2.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =2 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
