    "q-15742": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15742",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
