    "q-343680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343680",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/d4b4735b3154603284ed6f232ae640c5f47a536a5c26b75bb99970c8d0cb2c08.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $3$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $h(-4)=-6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-4) & =-6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ h(2) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+(5-3) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-1,-4\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
