    "q-343902": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343902",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
