      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$",
