    "q-316990": {
      "dom_id": "step-q316990",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196902/assets/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$",
